  "response": "Competing attributions of a grid failure are adjudicated on the physical record of the event rather than on the political claims that surround it: unit-by-unit outage reports, fuel-supply telemetry, equipment design specifications and the logged timeline of operator actions. Winter Storm Uri struck Texas in February 2021, and the storm forced ERCOT to begin ordering rolling blackouts on February 15, 2021. Those blackouts in turn deprived approximately 4.5 million Texas customers of electric service during the event. Wind generation on the ERCOT system reached a record output level in the days before the blackouts. The joint FERC-NERC report identified wind and solar generation as the primary cause of the outages.\n\nMonths after ERCOT began ordering those rolling blackouts, the FERC-NERC final report concluded that freezing and fuel-supply failures at thermal plants, rather than renewable generation, drove the supply shortfall; that conclusion and any assignment of primary blame to wind and solar generation cannot both be true. The outage accounting is what establishes the finding: freezing of generating equipment was the largest single cause of unplanned generator outages, which confirms that the shortfall originated in cold-exposed thermal capacity and its fuel chain. By contrast with the record renewable output of the preceding days, thermal units supplied the majority of the megawatts that went offline during the event; in other words, more than half of the lost generating capacity came from coal, gas, and nuclear plants.\n\nNatural gas deliveries to power plants were curtailed by freeze-offs in the production field, an outcome brought about by the sharp fall in Texas natural gas production as wellhead freeze-offs shut in supply. Critical instrumentation at generating units lacked cold-weather insulation and heat tracing. Every unit that dropped offline in the freeze was exposed to at least one of these two defects, the curtailment of gas in the production field or instrumentation left without insulation and heat tracing, and perhaps to both.\n\nInvestigators reconstruct a minute-by-minute grid frequency timeline to establish the sequence of failures. ERCOT system frequency fell to 57.5 Hz during the event. The adjudication therefore turns on matching each lost megawatt to a cause code, a fuel type and a timestamp, and on reading the fuel-supply record alongside the winterization standards in force at each plant. On that method, the megawatt ledger of February 2021 assigns the shortfall to an unwinterized thermal fleet and a frozen gas production system, and the record output of wind and solar in the days beforehand is a fact about the preceding week rather than an explanation of the collapse.",
